WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.260
KATIE INGHAM: Good evening.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.430
My name is Katie Ingham, and
I'm a junior biochemistry major

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.050
here at Hillsdale College.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.560
It is my pleasure to introduce
our speaker this evening.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.340
Darel E. Paul is a professor
of political science

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.660
at Williams College where
he has taught since 2001.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.010
A native of Minnesota,
he earned his BA and PhD

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:32.280
from the University of
Minnesota Twin Cities.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.340
He is a regular
contributor to First Things

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.040
and has written for
numerous other publications,

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.630
including the American Mind,
Collette, and Arrow magazine.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.850
He has also written
for academic journals

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.520
on class politics
in Western countries

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.140
and the manifestation of elite
ideologies and public policies.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.220
He is the author or co-author
of three books, including

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.390
the Theoretical Evolution of
International Political Economy

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:06.000
and From Toleration to
Equality, How Elites Brought

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.160
America To Same Sex Marriage.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.114
Please join me in
welcoming Darel E. Paul.

00:01:11.114 --> 00:01:14.078
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:30.900
DAREL E. PAUL: Good evening.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.330
As Katie mentioned to
you all a moment ago,

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.990
I'm a professor on the
faculty of Williams College.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:41.310
And according to US News
and World Report and Forbes

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.560
and the Wall Street Journal--

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.380
not Washington Monthly, but we
don't care what they think--

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.710
Williams is supposedly the
number one liberal arts

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.780
college in America.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.313
And one would expect then that
Williams being the number one

00:01:56.313 --> 00:01:58.980
liberal arts college in America,
one would expect then that when

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:02.190
Williams most recently went
through its accreditation

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:02.850
process--

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.370
it went through it most
recently four years ago--

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.680
those accreditors
would be duly impressed

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.800
with the institution's clear
definition of and commitment

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.710
to the liberal arts.

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.110
Alas this was not the case.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.720
I quote from their report.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.110
Quote, "we observed that
Williams faculty, staff,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.200
administrative leaders,
and students expressed

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.820
little consensus about what
the liberal arts is and does,

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.830
what it represents
on their campus, how

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.500
their approach intervenes
in the national discussion

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.620
of the liberal arts model,
opportunities for developing

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.320
a contemporary delivery
of the liberal arts,

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.770
and what leadership in
this sector might comprise.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:45.960
Consistent with the
highly decentralized

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.870
model of governance at
Williams, some faculty members

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.750
asserted that each department
and program develops

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:55.560
and implements its own vision of
the liberal arts, an assertion

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.110
that sits uneasily
alongside the claim

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.350
to the combined power
of depth and breadth

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.900
and of initiatives that
cut across the disciplines

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.270
and administrative divisions.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:07.950
More than one
professor responded

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.780
to the question about
the liberal arts

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.860
with a quip we know
it when we see it.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.160
Given the prominence of Williams
as a top ranked liberal arts

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.280
institution, the team
was struck by the absence

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.290
of a sharp articulation
of a forward

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:25.590
looking liberal arts mission
distinctive to Williams

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:28.080
both in the institution's
mission statement

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.970
and across dozens
of conversations

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:34.850
we conducted during our
time on campus," unquote.

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:38.390
Now I don't mean to pick on my
employer by bringing this up.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:40.310
I offer it instead as
a way of suggesting

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:43.430
that if the supposedly
number one liberal arts

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:47.030
college in America does not
know what the liberal arts are

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:50.638
or what the mission of a
liberal arts college should be,

00:03:50.638 --> 00:03:53.180
we shouldn't be at all surprised
if American higher education

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:57.570
more broadly is
just as confused.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.030
As you've already indulged
me in one Williams story,

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:03.060
I hope you'll be
patient with a second.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:06.630
Williams College
was founded in 1793.

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:10.080
At its bicentennial
celebration about 30 years ago,

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.470
the institution's eminent
historian Fred Rudolf

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.890
gave an address marking out
three eras through which

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:21.149
not only Williams but in the
view of Rudolf all elite higher

00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:22.860
education institutions
in America

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:25.710
had passed through
up to that time.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:30.270
The first he said was what he
called the Christian college.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:35.635
From the colonial period up to
really the late 19th century,

00:04:35.635 --> 00:04:37.260
the Christian college
was characterized

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:43.110
by a single classical curriculum
centered on ancient languages,

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:46.650
and in fact, being able to
pass an entrance exam in Latin

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:49.320
and/or Greek was
really kind of a marker

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.800
of that emphasis on a
classical education,

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.830
as well as mathematics,
logic, literature,

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:57.603
the physical sciences.

00:04:57.603 --> 00:04:59.520
There was, of course,
in the Christian college

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.990
mandatory chapel attendance to
cultivate a Christian spirit.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.260
The faculty was small
and primarily composed

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:10.447
of generalists rather
than specialists.

00:05:10.447 --> 00:05:12.030
The student body
certainly at Williams

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:16.650
was very much a local one, and
in fact in Rudolph's quote,

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:18.810
he says that
Williams at that time

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:22.740
was made up of, in his
words, poor boys of Calvinist

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:26.850
upbringing who could not
afford to go to Yale.

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:28.890
There is also a code
of conduct at what

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.980
Rudolf called the Christian
college encouraging deference

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:36.480
to authority and the curbing
of eccentric or nonconformist

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.580
behavior.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.830
By the late 19th
century all this

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:44.280
had transformed into the
second era, what Rudolf

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:48.030
called the gentleman's college.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:49.870
What characterized a
gentleman's college?

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:52.320
Things like a loosening
of the strictures

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.950
of a classical curriculum
piece by piece.

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:57.990
In fact, at Williams at
least the Latin requirement

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.480
was abolished for
the class of 1938.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:04.440
No one need any longer
complete Latin to graduate

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.270
from the institution.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:09.360
The student body went from
being a much local one, right,

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.060
the poor boys that couldn't
afford to go to Yale to a more

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:16.560
regional student body throughout
the Northeast and the Midwest.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.860
In an era of mass primary
and increasingly mass high

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:22.950
school education, the
gentleman's student body

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:26.610
cultivated by receiving students
from college prep schools

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:31.080
at various parts of those
regions in the country.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:33.030
It was also characterized
by the cultivation

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:36.720
of what you might call
gentlemanly values, especially

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.630
through fraternities
and athletic teams,

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:42.570
as well as a sense of
personal honor and a deference

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:44.190
to hierarchy.

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:46.470
And finally, the
growing support staff

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:49.770
of the gentleman's
college was characteristic

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:53.010
and an increasingly large
bureaucracy befitting one

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:56.390
might say a gentleman's club.

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:58.460
In the wake of
World War II, Rudolf

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:03.950
observed the emergence of what
he called the consumer college

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:06.280
opening colleges and
universities to the masses

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:09.380
after World War I and
catering to their interests

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:11.930
in civic and
professional advancement

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:14.090
much more so than we
might say cultivation

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:16.070
of their characters.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:18.440
By this time by the
1940s and 1950s,

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:21.650
we have entered into a period
of universal high school

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:22.670
education.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:25.143
So therefore, the students
who might go on to college

00:07:25.143 --> 00:07:26.810
were looking for an
experience much more

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:30.530
similar to their high
schools than certainly

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:35.260
private schools like Williams
were offering until that time.

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:39.080
At Williams at least, mandatory
chapel ended in the 1950s,

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:42.200
and fraternities were
eliminated at Williams

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:46.700
in 1961, which I would say are
the two in rapid succession

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:49.520
symbolic deaths, if you
will, of both the Christian

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:52.400
and the gentleman's college.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:55.640
The college goes
coed in about 1970,

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:58.400
as many schools did in the
United States, private ones

00:07:58.400 --> 00:07:59.570
in particular.

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:02.510
Increasingly,
liberal or one might

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:04.250
choose the word
libertine environment

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:06.320
began to be predominant.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:09.110
There's an old joke, a
characteristic of the consumer

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:12.080
college that the job
of a college president

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:14.840
is to provide sex
for the students,

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:16.760
a winning football
team for the alums,

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:20.690
and parking for the faculty.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:24.050
Williams also became, as
most elite institutions

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:28.220
of higher education at this time
became, a national institution

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:31.160
and in Rudolph's terms a,
quote, "national institution

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:33.610
going global."

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:37.100
And as well, it was a
response to market forces.

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:40.700
The growth of increasingly
lavish facilities,

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:43.429
larger and larger bureaucracies,
providing services

00:08:43.429 --> 00:08:45.710
of all sorts, the
end of really what

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:48.740
could be called any kind
of coherent curriculum

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:51.410
in terms of to be replaced
with a essentially

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:54.860
do it yourself education.

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:58.280
One element of Rudolph's take
on the consumer college, though,

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:01.520
in my view is overly naive.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:04.720
What Rudolf thought the consumer
college of the post-world War

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.010
II period was about
was in his words--

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:09.920
this is a quote from
his work, a fulfillment

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.830
of the Democratic
promise of America,

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:16.460
or elsewhere he talks about the
Democratic tolerant and diverse

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:19.970
culture that characterizes
the consumer college.

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:23.210
In fact, in his speech
at the 200th anniversary

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:25.250
of Williams College
in the early 1990s,

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:30.010
he said Tocqueville himself
to defend this view.

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:33.100
But rather than a great
Democratic wave washing

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.643
over the elitist world of
the gentleman's college,

00:09:36.643 --> 00:09:38.560
I think the consumer
college is better thought

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:42.220
of as the meritocratic college.

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:45.550
This is the college we're
all familiar with today,

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:48.160
one in which excellence
excellent performance

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:52.330
on the SAT or a wealth of
advanced placement courses

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:55.150
or a long resume
probably accumulated

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:56.710
since middle school
demonstrating

00:09:56.710 --> 00:10:02.360
one's capacity for leadership
is the price of admissions.

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:06.090
My point in walking through
this history is twofold.

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:08.390
The first is to demonstrate
that the mission and even

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:10.700
the definition of
the liberal arts

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:15.560
has changed markedly over
the last 200 plus years.

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:18.650
Who is to be served,
the ideal of character

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:22.720
to be formed, the institutional
embodiment of learning,

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:24.740
each has transformed
higher education and what

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:28.010
we take to be the liberal arts.

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:30.020
The second is to
suggest that America

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:34.310
may be on the cusp of
a fourth era, a post

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:37.640
meritocratic one defined
by a new curriculum,

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:41.870
new standards of achievement,
a new ideal of character,

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:44.060
and a new social
purpose generally going

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:45.020
under the name--

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:46.370
at least certainly at Williams--

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:49.670
diversity, equity,
and inclusion or more

00:10:49.670 --> 00:10:51.575
succinctly social justice.

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:58.160
Fred Rudolf, the
Williams historian

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:01.760
that I've been quoting, his
sketch of higher education

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:06.080
is really a small part of a much
larger tale of the evolution

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:08.270
of the American elite.

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:12.290
Higher education today tends to
be rather shy about this role

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:14.270
that it serves in
our society that

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:19.855
is to admit that its mission
is to cultivate an elite.

00:11:19.855 --> 00:11:21.980
It tends to be much more
comfortable with the claim

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:26.060
that what the college does is
to develop, quote, "leaders,"

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:27.530
unquote.

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:30.200
But what else is a
leader other than someone

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:34.280
of superlative ability and
accomplishment and leaders

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:38.300
as a group constituted
by such persons?

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:40.580
Dare I suggest that
such a group might

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:44.450
be considered to be the best
in their respective endeavors

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:49.640
or in Greek an aristoi, who
together lead or govern as,

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:52.550
of course, an aristocracy.

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:56.120
Now I think we shouldn't
recoil from this thought.

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:58.760
While the specifics of
any particular elite

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:01.490
may exhibit much
to be condemned,

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:04.910
such an elite very existence
is neither damnable nor really

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:07.250
quite frankly preventable.

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:10.010
Not simply difference
but inequality

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:14.990
is written into the fabric of
human nature and human society.

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:17.000
What we should care
about, therefore, I

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:20.780
contend is not whether
we have an elite or not--

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:22.580
we shall have one--

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:26.480
but rather that elite's quality.

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:28.610
Thomas Jefferson
offers, in my view,

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:31.760
at least a very fruitful way
of thinking about the existence

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:34.730
and role of a social elite.

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:37.940
In his famous work Notes
on the State of Virginia,

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:41.750
Jefferson advocated a system of
education that would cultivate

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:43.860
not only the
population in general

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:46.820
but also an elite who
would occupy the most

00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:49.768
prominent positions in society.

00:12:49.768 --> 00:12:51.560
If you know Notes on
the State of Virginia,

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:53.930
this will all be, of
course, familiar to you.

00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:56.540
Jefferson recommended
in that work

00:12:56.540 --> 00:13:00.230
free universal education
at an elementary level

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:03.050
in every locale
throughout the state

00:13:03.050 --> 00:13:05.090
to be complemented
by a winnowing

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:08.670
process in which the student
in Jefferson's words,

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:12.620
quote, "of the best
genius in the school"

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:17.060
would be promoted on at public
expense to grammar schools.

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:18.650
After that another
winnowing process

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:20.450
would take place at
the grammar school

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:22.370
where only the best
students therefore

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:25.580
would be continued on
again at public cost.

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:28.140
Certainly if you were able to
pay for this, you could stay,

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:31.850
but this was a way essentially
to cultivate the talents

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:34.220
and discover them,
quite frankly, of those

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:36.080
who could not pay.

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:38.510
At the end of grammar
school, a final culling

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:41.780
would take place in which the
most accomplished students

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:44.900
would be sent on to positions
at the state university,

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:48.320
again, on public scholarships.

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:51.560
Jefferson summed up his vision
by describing it thusly,

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:55.160
and I quote, "the general
objects of this law

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:58.190
are to provide an education
adapt to the years,

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:01.850
to the capacity, and the
condition of everyone

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:06.670
and directed to their
freedom and happiness."

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:09.340
But this isn't the only
goal that Jefferson

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:11.810
had for education.

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:13.510
In fact, in his
famous correspondence

00:14:13.510 --> 00:14:17.300
with his longtime political
nemesis John Adams,

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:19.060
Jefferson likewise
praised the role

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:23.782
of what he referred to as
the natural aristocracy.

00:14:23.782 --> 00:14:25.240
Jefferson, of
course, distinguished

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:27.850
what he called the natural
aristocracy from what he called

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:30.130
the artificial aristocracy--

00:14:30.130 --> 00:14:32.230
the artificial
aristocracy more sort

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:35.020
of in the sense that
we generally understand

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:38.440
the term aristocracy
someone born to a position

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:43.120
rather than someone who has we
might say earned that position.

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:45.250
But the definition
that Jefferson

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:48.430
gave to what he called
the natural aristocracy

00:14:48.430 --> 00:14:50.020
was not simply one
of intelligence.

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:51.850
It was not simply
one of as he says

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:56.350
in Notes on the State of
Virginia one of genius.

00:14:56.350 --> 00:14:59.020
Instead Jefferson
asserts that the grounds

00:14:59.020 --> 00:15:01.630
of this natural
aristocracy among men

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:07.480
are, quote, "virtue
and talents."

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:11.230
One would hope, therefore, that
the liberal arts are training

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:13.510
not simply in talent
and its cultivation

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:17.310
but likewise in virtue.

00:15:17.310 --> 00:15:20.950
The ancient meaning of the word
liberal from which liberal arts

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:24.850
takes its name partakes
not simply of a breadth

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:27.370
of knowledge-- although
it certainly does that--

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:33.700
but also a generosity of spirit
and a nobility of character.

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:37.450
The liberal arts thus
from its very origins

00:15:37.450 --> 00:15:41.710
is a realm of knowledge and
a mode of education befitting

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:45.290
the free person, the person
at liberty, if you will,

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:49.720
one unconstrained by
necessity, an education one

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:54.550
might say proper
to a true aristoi.

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:58.630
Now fears that American
elites are lacking in virtue

00:15:58.630 --> 00:16:01.360
is as old as the Republic.

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:03.490
I mentioned a moment ago
the famous correspondence

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:05.710
between Adams and Jefferson.

00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:09.190
Adams himself insisted that
Jefferson's distinction

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:12.080
between the artificial
aristocracy, on the one hand,

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:16.090
the natural aristocracy on the
other was in his words, quote,

00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:18.850
"not well-founded."

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:22.750
Talent in Adam's view
was too diverse to find

00:16:22.750 --> 00:16:27.688
a common definition, and virtue
in Adams rather pessimistic

00:16:27.688 --> 00:16:29.980
view-- and if you know anything
about Adams' character,

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:32.860
this won't be surprising
to you at all.

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:35.290
He thought virtue was
destined to be corrupted

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:38.470
as the artificial
aristocracy falls

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:41.880
into monarchy and despotism.

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:45.330
In our own time, books
are published every year

00:16:45.330 --> 00:16:48.990
lamenting the corruption
of America's elite.

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:52.110
In fact, you can't swing
a dead sheepskin today

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:56.935
without hitting a damning
critique of meritocracy.

00:16:56.935 --> 00:16:58.560
And in fact, in just
the last 18 months

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:00.925
these eight titles
have hit the shelves.

00:17:00.925 --> 00:17:02.550
You may be familiar
with some of these,

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:05.170
maybe even have read them.

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:08.369
We have Daniel
Markovitz book, quote,

00:17:08.369 --> 00:17:11.550
that is titled The Meritocracy
Trap, How America's

00:17:11.550 --> 00:17:15.240
Foundational Myth Feeds
Inequality, Dismantles

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:18.599
the Middle Class, and
Devours the Elite.

00:17:18.599 --> 00:17:23.310
We have Michael Lind's book, The
New Class War, Saving Democracy

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:27.060
from the Managerial
Elite, Joel Kotkin,

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:30.510
The Coming of Neofuedalism, A
Warning to the Global Middle

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:35.250
Class, Anthony Carnevale's
The Merit Myth,

00:17:35.250 --> 00:17:40.410
How our Colleges Favor the Rich
and Divide America, Freddie

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:44.310
deBoer's, The Cult of Smart,
How our Broken Education System

00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:49.710
Perpetuates Social Injustice,
David Goodhart's, Head Hand

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:53.730
Heart, Why Intelligence is
Over Rewarded, Manual Workers

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:57.810
Matter and Caregivers
Deserve more Respect,

00:17:57.810 --> 00:18:00.540
Michael Sandel's The
Tyranny of Merit What's

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:03.810
Become of the Common
Good, and finally Peter

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:07.560
Mandler's The Crisis
of the Meritocracy,

00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:10.470
Britain's Transition to Mass
Education since the Second

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:11.250
World War.

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:12.667
These are all books
that have just

00:18:12.667 --> 00:18:14.190
been produced in
the last 18 months

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:18.500
on essentially the same topic,
a critique of the elite,

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:21.590
a critique of our meritocrats.

00:18:21.590 --> 00:18:23.340
It's fair to say that
America's positively

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:27.540
swimming in condemnation
of its leadership class,

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:29.672
and it's not simply from
one political perspective.

00:18:29.672 --> 00:18:30.880
We can find it from the left.

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:33.498
We can find it from
the right as well.

00:18:33.498 --> 00:18:35.040
These are the people
who are supposed

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:39.350
to be our best, our aristoi.

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:41.970
The meritocratic system
created after World War II

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:45.420
was supposed to be an
improvement upon what

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:47.850
in Rudolph's terms was,
of course, the gentleman's

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:51.330
college and the economic and
political system over which

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:53.580
those gentlemen ruled.

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:57.090
But it seems instead that the
social consensus on both sides

00:18:57.090 --> 00:18:58.260
of the political--

00:18:58.260 --> 00:19:01.350
excuse me, political spectrum
is that this great effort

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:03.075
has been largely a failure.

00:19:06.290 --> 00:19:11.580
I think a good instance
of this happened in 2018.

00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:13.620
Ross Douthat, who as
you probably know,

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:16.440
is a columnist at
The New York Times,

00:19:16.440 --> 00:19:19.180
sort of, a minor
controversy on this theme.

00:19:19.180 --> 00:19:21.960
In 2018, when George
Herbert Walker Bush,

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:25.680
the former president died,
Douthat published an opinion

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:31.730
column, and the title of that
column was "We Miss the WASPs."

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:33.240
It wasn't why we should.

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:34.260
it wasn't we will.

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:35.250
It was we do right now.

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:36.060
We miss them.

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:39.540
We miss the WASP-- that is the
white Anglo-Saxon Protestant

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:43.170
gentleman who earned
their C's or maybe they

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:45.720
earned better grades
than that at Yale

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:48.270
and went on to run the
country's major corporations

00:19:48.270 --> 00:19:50.340
and governmental
institutions as,

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:53.760
of course, George HW
Bush himself had done.

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:59.040
Because our contemporary
meritocrats in contrast--

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:00.810
and this is Douthat's
words-- quote,

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:06.900
"rule us neither as wisely
nor as well," unquote.

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:08.960
Just to give you kind
of a brief synopsis,

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:11.840
Douthat's argument
essentially went like this.

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:14.510
Our currently elite,
the elite of 2018,

00:20:14.510 --> 00:20:17.700
the elite of the recent
period, if you will,

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:21.260
is just as animated as
any elite has ever been.

00:20:21.260 --> 00:20:24.440
But power seeking is just as
exclusionary as the WASPs were,

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:27.800
perhaps, on different grounds
just as self-replicating within

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:32.180
families, but according
to Douthat at least,

00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:36.590
the WASPs had something that our
current mayor autocrats don't.

00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:39.470
And his claim was
that they had virtue.

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:42.500
That is they had
noblesse oblige.

00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:44.780
They had an ethic of
personal service--

00:20:44.780 --> 00:20:48.590
or sorry an ethic of service,
also an ethic of personal

00:20:48.590 --> 00:20:53.600
austerity, of self-discipline,
and a form of cosmopolitanism

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:57.470
that didn't sort of descend
into simply sort of the lack

00:20:57.470 --> 00:20:59.720
of rootedness--

00:20:59.720 --> 00:21:02.390
being a world citizen we
might call the alternative.

00:21:02.390 --> 00:21:04.490
Douthat called this
in his words, quote,

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:08.010
"an aristocratic
spirit," unquote.

00:21:08.010 --> 00:21:10.220
So I think in
Douthat's view what

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:13.520
the WASPs had that
the meritocratic don't

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:17.600
is virtue, which realizes
itself in competence

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:20.090
and effectiveness.

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:22.340
And what that competence
combined with virtue

00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:28.740
produces in Douthat's view
is legitimacy and trust.

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:30.630
Now as you can imagine,
Douthat's column

00:21:30.630 --> 00:21:33.705
was widely criticized,
and it was widely

00:21:33.705 --> 00:21:34.830
criticized on the grounds--

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:39.260
I bet all of you can predict
before I even tell you--

00:21:39.260 --> 00:21:41.610
Douthat's column was bigoted.

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:42.930
It was racist.

00:21:42.930 --> 00:21:46.110
It was white supremacist.

00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:49.770
But most of the criticism of
Douthat's argument back in 2018

00:21:49.770 --> 00:21:52.500
at the death of George
Herbert Walker Bush

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:56.040
really sidestepped I think
the explicit comparison

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:58.800
that Douthat encouraged
us to do to engage

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:02.460
in between this past
and this current elite

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:06.780
and ultimately to engage in that
very question of competence,

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:11.730
effectiveness,
legitimacy, trust.

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:14.440
That was really at the
heart of Douthat's argument.

00:22:14.440 --> 00:22:17.550
And instead they simply favorite
a condemnation on Douthat

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:21.290
on the grounds of
social justice.

00:22:21.290 --> 00:22:22.820
By this time in my talk--

00:22:22.820 --> 00:22:24.570
I've seen about 20 minutes in--

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:27.080
it's probably appropriate
for me to define my terms

00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:28.550
a little more clearly.

00:22:28.550 --> 00:22:33.410
By social justice so-called,
I mean two related things.

00:22:33.410 --> 00:22:40.250
First, an ideology or perhaps
we might say a religion.

00:22:40.250 --> 00:22:42.410
Some of you may know
a recent book came out

00:22:42.410 --> 00:22:44.660
last year called
Cynical Theories

00:22:44.660 --> 00:22:46.550
by a pair of academics--

00:22:46.550 --> 00:22:50.850
former academics Helen
Pluckrose and James Lindsay,

00:22:50.850 --> 00:22:54.500
and they argue that social
justice or postmodernism

00:22:54.500 --> 00:22:57.290
is characterized by what they
call the postmodern knowledge

00:22:57.290 --> 00:23:00.440
principle and the postmodern
political or sorry power

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:01.580
principle.

00:23:01.580 --> 00:23:03.470
The knowledge
principle fundamentally

00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:08.090
is what can be known,
and it is highly oriented

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:10.460
towards subjectivism
and relativism.

00:23:10.460 --> 00:23:12.440
The power principle
essentially says

00:23:12.440 --> 00:23:16.070
that the only thing that really
constitutes human society

00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:19.460
is power, and the
exercise of that power

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:22.730
by group A over groups B
through whatever letter

00:23:22.730 --> 00:23:24.980
you might choose
in the alphabet.

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:29.970
And really there is nothing else
in society except for power.

00:23:29.970 --> 00:23:33.050
So let's on the one hand so this
if you might say free floating

00:23:33.050 --> 00:23:38.030
ideology, but the second
thing I mean by social justice

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:44.960
is the ideology or faith, if
you will, of the American elite.

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:48.020
That is the system
of values and dogmas

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:51.170
that empowers what is
quite frankly a younger

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:53.660
somewhat browner but
still mostly white

00:23:53.660 --> 00:23:59.180
governing class to both displace
an older, whiter but still

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:02.810
somewhat brown elite
and exercise power

00:24:02.810 --> 00:24:05.450
over the rest of society.

00:24:05.450 --> 00:24:07.310
And thus, therefore,
what social justice

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:11.370
is not just simply a
free-floating ideology,

00:24:11.370 --> 00:24:14.240
but also an instrument of
what I certainly will happily

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:18.860
call class struggle both within
the confines of the elite

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:20.570
as well as outside of it.

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:30.030
Seeing social justice as
a class ideology, I think,

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:33.080
helps us to understand
its power and how

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:36.380
it marks a changing of the
guard as it worked from one era

00:24:36.380 --> 00:24:40.400
to another, one
elite to another.

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:42.830
In some way social
justice is a reaction

00:24:42.830 --> 00:24:47.900
against meritocracy and against
meritocracy's shortcomings.

00:24:47.900 --> 00:24:52.610
In reaction against an elite
brimming with intelligence but,

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:54.560
perhaps, a
questionable deployment

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:57.170
of their talent, social
justice weighs heavily

00:24:57.170 --> 00:25:01.820
on what it takes to be virtue
or, perhaps, social justice

00:25:01.820 --> 00:25:05.270
adherence might prefer
to call it moral clarity

00:25:05.270 --> 00:25:10.730
while largely dispensing with
talent as an elitist myth.

00:25:10.730 --> 00:25:14.250
So when I suggest that social
justice is a class religion

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:16.310
or class faith, I
don't do so lightly.

00:25:16.310 --> 00:25:18.200
I certainly don't
mean it as some kind

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:21.740
of veiled insult the way
that many secularist critics

00:25:21.740 --> 00:25:25.040
of social justice do use
it as simply a way to kind

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:29.240
of tar social justice as
dogmatic or blind faith

00:25:29.240 --> 00:25:31.820
or overly simplistic.

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:36.920
Social justice, I contend, is as
much a religion as Christianity

00:25:36.920 --> 00:25:40.910
or communism or the therapeutic.

00:25:40.910 --> 00:25:43.580
I'll get to that latter point.

00:25:43.580 --> 00:25:46.070
And being a class,
religion, or perhaps

00:25:46.070 --> 00:25:49.400
I should say a class
inflected religion much as

00:25:49.400 --> 00:25:53.270
say Episcopalian more
or Pentecostalism

00:25:53.270 --> 00:25:57.500
have their obvious class
inflected characteristics, as

00:25:57.500 --> 00:26:02.390
with any successful faith,
social justice shows every sign

00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:04.730
of capturing the
country's institutions

00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:08.300
of political and
economic and social power

00:26:08.300 --> 00:26:12.960
and thus of making itself
an established faith.

00:26:12.960 --> 00:26:16.650
We can see this, I think,
veiled in Fred Rudolph's three

00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:19.770
eras of higher education and
therefore his three colleges,

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:22.830
if you will, from
American history.

00:26:22.830 --> 00:26:25.350
From the foundations of the
country in the colonial period

00:26:25.350 --> 00:26:27.300
all the way down to
the late 19th century,

00:26:27.300 --> 00:26:30.340
America's colleges
were Christian.

00:26:30.340 --> 00:26:30.840
Why?

00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:34.920
Because America's elite
was a Protestant elite.

00:26:34.920 --> 00:26:36.930
Conservative
Protestant morality was

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:39.840
taught in the public
schools, as well as

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:43.080
obviously in the institutions
of higher education.

00:26:43.080 --> 00:26:45.810
Conservative Protestant churches
were central institutions

00:26:45.810 --> 00:26:47.160
of socialization.

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:49.830
Conservative Protestant
clergy played prominent roles

00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:51.780
in the moral life
of the country.

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:53.880
Nearly all public
debate took place

00:26:53.880 --> 00:26:56.490
within the contours of
conservative Protestant

00:26:56.490 --> 00:26:58.740
sensibilities.

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:01.500
After the Civil
War, the influence

00:27:01.500 --> 00:27:04.830
of this Protestant
establishment began to wane.

00:27:04.830 --> 00:27:07.830
A new secular
university model emerged

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:11.520
supported by the rising titans
of industrial capitalism.

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:14.700
The academy turned to
research rather than

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:16.560
character formation.

00:27:16.560 --> 00:27:19.950
New professions emerged, such
as psychiatry and social work,

00:27:19.950 --> 00:27:22.530
which sometimes were
under the sponsorship

00:27:22.530 --> 00:27:24.480
of Protestant
seminaries eager to draw

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:28.080
upon the new prestige of
science and academic training

00:27:28.080 --> 00:27:31.950
but over time eventually
displaced the clergy

00:27:31.950 --> 00:27:36.300
and began to rival them,
as well as the institutions

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:38.910
of the professional class
began to rival churches

00:27:38.910 --> 00:27:40.920
and their ministers.

00:27:40.920 --> 00:27:44.190
Conservative Protestants
by the early 20th century

00:27:44.190 --> 00:27:48.270
found themselves tagged
as fundamentalists.

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:53.010
The WASP gentleman was entering
into his spotlight constructing

00:27:53.010 --> 00:27:56.490
on his way both economic
and political empires.

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:59.460
His liberal Protestantism
as contrasted

00:27:59.460 --> 00:28:01.800
to the conservative
Protestantism that

00:28:01.800 --> 00:28:06.890
came before blurred into an
imminent religion of humanity

00:28:06.890 --> 00:28:10.710
interested less in righteousness
and salvation and much more so

00:28:10.710 --> 00:28:15.300
in social responsibility
and material progress.

00:28:15.300 --> 00:28:18.600
But this era too, of
course, fell into eclipse.

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:22.298
After World War II, the
meritocracy began to emerge.

00:28:22.298 --> 00:28:24.090
The number of students
sitting for the SAT,

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:29.430
for example, increased 10-fold
over the course of the 1950s.

00:28:29.430 --> 00:28:31.770
The Civil Rights movement
and the women's movements

00:28:31.770 --> 00:28:33.900
of the '60s and
'70s quite literally

00:28:33.900 --> 00:28:37.380
changed the face
of America's elite.

00:28:37.380 --> 00:28:40.260
By the 1980s, diversity
had become higher

00:28:40.260 --> 00:28:42.840
education's cultural
project and its hoped

00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:44.600
for source of legitimacy.

00:28:47.110 --> 00:28:50.230
But rather than tag the
college of the meritocratz

00:28:50.230 --> 00:28:52.250
as one defined by an
act of consumption--

00:28:52.250 --> 00:28:54.490
this is Rudolph's
term of course--

00:28:54.490 --> 00:28:57.760
I think we can see that the
deeper culture of what Rudolf

00:28:57.760 --> 00:29:01.210
called the consumer college
or what I've been calling

00:29:01.210 --> 00:29:05.530
the meritocracy is therapeutic.

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:09.580
It expresses the values
of individual success

00:29:09.580 --> 00:29:12.550
as well as the compensatory--

00:29:12.550 --> 00:29:14.620
and as well as
being compensatory--

00:29:14.620 --> 00:29:17.800
excuse me-- of the
psychological costs

00:29:17.800 --> 00:29:20.800
of this intense
competition and striving

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:23.710
that defines the meritocrat.

00:29:23.710 --> 00:29:26.410
The therapeutic is
defined by its commitments

00:29:26.410 --> 00:29:31.990
to three core values,
individuality, authenticity,

00:29:31.990 --> 00:29:33.340
and liberation.

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:35.800
Let me speak to those
just very briefly.

00:29:35.800 --> 00:29:39.970
Individuality is not exactly
the same thing as individualism.

00:29:39.970 --> 00:29:42.760
It takes traditional
American individualism,

00:29:42.760 --> 00:29:48.400
but it weds that individualism
to a romantic sensibility

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:51.670
of the self as a unique
and creative spirit whose

00:29:51.670 --> 00:29:55.900
reason for existence
is its own expression.

00:29:55.900 --> 00:30:00.220
Authenticity therefore
builds upon individuality

00:30:00.220 --> 00:30:04.450
as the public expression of
each person's unique essence.

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:06.670
To be authentic,
one must neither

00:30:06.670 --> 00:30:09.370
lie about nor obscure
one's inner life.

00:30:09.370 --> 00:30:12.020
Therefore, one must
publicly proclaim it.

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:13.150
Thank God for social media.

00:30:13.150 --> 00:30:16.420
Now we can all proclaim
our individual inner lives

00:30:16.420 --> 00:30:17.980
to everyone.

00:30:17.980 --> 00:30:22.270
And therefore the feelings
and desires, imaginations

00:30:22.270 --> 00:30:26.740
and experiences are to be
announced to the world,

00:30:26.740 --> 00:30:30.460
the therapeutic self, if
you will, is to be revealed.

00:30:30.460 --> 00:30:35.380
This inner life must be brought
forward and outward expressed

00:30:35.380 --> 00:30:38.350
or one might even say from
a post-modern perspective

00:30:38.350 --> 00:30:42.550
performed in words and deeds
and confessions and self

00:30:42.550 --> 00:30:43.900
revelations.

00:30:43.900 --> 00:30:49.930
Authenticity urges one ever
onward to quote "be yourself"

00:30:49.930 --> 00:30:53.600
and to quote "speak your truth."

00:30:53.600 --> 00:30:58.520
Finally, liberation, liberation
is the wider social condition

00:30:58.520 --> 00:31:03.080
in which these authentic selves
become socially recognized.

00:31:03.080 --> 00:31:06.440
Mental health professionals
once counseled some decades

00:31:06.440 --> 00:31:10.400
ago the development of
prosocial interdictions that

00:31:10.400 --> 00:31:12.350
would enable an
individual's adaptation

00:31:12.350 --> 00:31:14.240
to social expectations.

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:17.480
But under the therapeutic, what
mental health professionals--

00:31:17.480 --> 00:31:20.750
at least the ones who are most
prominent in their fields--

00:31:20.750 --> 00:31:23.240
now they advocate for the
wholesale transformation

00:31:23.240 --> 00:31:27.290
of society so that
society can facilitate

00:31:27.290 --> 00:31:32.370
the self-actualization
of each individual.

00:31:32.370 --> 00:31:34.260
Social justice, I
think, is very much

00:31:34.260 --> 00:31:37.440
an outgrowth of the
therapeutic but it

00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:41.400
is inflected through
intersectionality

00:31:41.400 --> 00:31:43.740
to be the qualities
rather of individuals

00:31:43.740 --> 00:31:48.150
and individual personalities
to be the qualities of identity

00:31:48.150 --> 00:31:52.290
groups and therefore, of course,
the individuals who constitute

00:31:52.290 --> 00:31:54.870
those identity groups.

00:31:54.870 --> 00:31:57.450
I'm assuming we're all
quite familiar, perhaps,

00:31:57.450 --> 00:32:01.260
overly familiar with the
doctrine of intersectionality.

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:04.300
So those identity
categories of race,

00:32:04.300 --> 00:32:08.850
sex, color, sexual orientation,
gender identity, disability

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:12.610
status, et cetera,
et cetera, et cetera,

00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:15.060
they're really the building
blocks from this perspective

00:32:15.060 --> 00:32:16.380
of society.

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:19.470
That is this is the social
justice ontology, what

00:32:19.470 --> 00:32:22.110
really exists in the world.

00:32:22.110 --> 00:32:26.640
Each category says social
justice has its own experience

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:29.940
of the world, its own
knowledge, if you will,

00:32:29.940 --> 00:32:32.370
and therefore its
own way of knowing

00:32:32.370 --> 00:32:34.710
that exists within
what we might call

00:32:34.710 --> 00:32:38.340
an inverted hierarchy in
which those who it's claimed

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:40.530
suffer the most
oppression are, therefore,

00:32:40.530 --> 00:32:43.320
the most knowledgeable and
those who suffer the least

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:47.340
oppression, therefore, are, in
fact, the least knowledgeable.

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:49.860
This is the social
justice epistemology

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:53.880
that is a way of knowing,
which comes to social justice

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:57.300
through post-structuralism,
back to Antonio Gramsci, Marx,

00:32:57.300 --> 00:33:01.710
and really all the way back
to Hegel and his master slave

00:33:01.710 --> 00:33:03.510
dialectic.

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:07.365
Thus social justice shares
with the therapeutic

00:33:07.365 --> 00:33:10.680
the sensibility of right
of radical subjectivity

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:14.130
in which what you and I
might call the real world

00:33:14.130 --> 00:33:19.020
beyond ourselves and shared
with all others does not exist

00:33:19.020 --> 00:33:23.070
or at least does not exist
in any meaningful sense.

00:33:23.070 --> 00:33:25.650
The claim, of course, is
that everything that exists

00:33:25.650 --> 00:33:29.280
can only be known by human
beings through language,

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:31.800
and therefore, the real
world, if we can call it that,

00:33:31.800 --> 00:33:35.610
can never really be known
outside of human language,

00:33:35.610 --> 00:33:39.630
and the construction therefore
of the real world so-called

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:43.980
by the exercise of power
all the way down even

00:33:43.980 --> 00:33:46.980
into the roots of our
humanity, our material

00:33:46.980 --> 00:33:50.990
bodies, our rational souls.

00:33:50.990 --> 00:33:56.360
Thus we come to social justice's
political principle, a doctrine

00:33:56.360 --> 00:33:58.730
that asserts the
human experience is

00:33:58.730 --> 00:34:03.260
a wholly imminent one
of material, cultural,

00:34:03.260 --> 00:34:07.300
and psychological
oppression and liberation.

00:34:07.300 --> 00:34:11.110
The utopia of social justice
is the same as the utopia

00:34:11.110 --> 00:34:15.699
of the therapeutic, equal
esteem and equal social

00:34:15.699 --> 00:34:21.260
recognition, which we now
seem to be calling equity.

00:34:21.260 --> 00:34:24.710
One can see this I think clearly
in the best selling racialist

00:34:24.710 --> 00:34:27.949
book of the
post-George Floyd era,

00:34:27.949 --> 00:34:32.070
Ibram X. Kennedy's How
to be an Antiracist.

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:34.230
I assume many of you
have heard of this.

00:34:34.230 --> 00:34:37.010
Some of you may
even have read it.

00:34:37.010 --> 00:34:39.469
This book relates
the author's lie

00:34:39.469 --> 00:34:43.429
about autobiographical journey
of cultural and psychological

00:34:43.429 --> 00:34:49.040
struggle to shed judgment and
embrace cultural relativism.

00:34:49.040 --> 00:34:53.750
The dream is equity through
all the institutions

00:34:53.750 --> 00:34:55.592
of social order.

00:34:55.592 --> 00:34:57.050
This includes the
state, of course,

00:34:57.050 --> 00:35:00.832
but it's not limited
importantly to the state.

00:35:00.832 --> 00:35:02.540
And among other sites
of power, the state

00:35:02.540 --> 00:35:05.970
really is hardly the
most important of all.

00:35:05.970 --> 00:35:08.900
I think we saw this very clearly
in the four years of the Donald

00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:10.580
Trump presidency.

00:35:10.580 --> 00:35:12.770
While social justice
was marginalized

00:35:12.770 --> 00:35:15.620
in the state throughout
those four years,

00:35:15.620 --> 00:35:19.250
it only grew stronger
outside the state.

00:35:19.250 --> 00:35:21.230
And its return under
the Biden administration

00:35:21.230 --> 00:35:24.710
is really about a capstone
now to its conquest

00:35:24.710 --> 00:35:27.710
of every institution of
elite power and authority

00:35:27.710 --> 00:35:31.760
in the country, the press,
the professions especially

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:36.260
law and medicine, the culture
industries, Silicon Valley,

00:35:36.260 --> 00:35:40.570
Wall Street, higher education.

00:35:40.570 --> 00:35:42.840
As the emphasis of this
conference, of course,

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:44.860
is higher education,
let me say something

00:35:44.860 --> 00:35:48.700
briefly on the matter of
the academy in particular.

00:35:48.700 --> 00:35:52.440
Social justice intends
to do to higher education

00:35:52.440 --> 00:35:57.490
with Soviet communism to
define art, literature, music.

00:35:57.490 --> 00:36:01.540
In the USSR under officially
approved socialist realism,

00:36:01.540 --> 00:36:03.400
art was judged
first and foremost

00:36:03.400 --> 00:36:08.320
by how well it depicted Soviet
ideals, how well it parroted

00:36:08.320 --> 00:36:12.550
Communist Party doctrine, and
how well it cultivated loyalty

00:36:12.550 --> 00:36:14.290
to the Soviet system.

00:36:14.290 --> 00:36:16.720
Not even the physical
sciences were

00:36:16.720 --> 00:36:20.890
exempted from serving a
primarily ideological purpose

00:36:20.890 --> 00:36:23.020
during the 30-year
reign of Lysenkoism

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:26.410
over Soviet biology
and agronomy.

00:36:26.410 --> 00:36:30.070
Substitute critical race
theory for Marxism, Leninism.

00:36:30.070 --> 00:36:31.990
Substitute whiteness
for capitalism

00:36:31.990 --> 00:36:34.930
substitute racial
justice for dictatorship

00:36:34.930 --> 00:36:36.880
of the proletariat,
and I think you'll

00:36:36.880 --> 00:36:42.890
understand much of what
social justice offers us.

00:36:42.890 --> 00:36:46.310
This is the faith of our elites.

00:36:46.310 --> 00:36:50.720
Consider the mantra
diversity, equity, inclusion--

00:36:50.720 --> 00:36:54.590
it's become the operating
faith of not all certainly,

00:36:54.590 --> 00:36:58.940
as you well know, but almost all
of American higher education.

00:36:58.940 --> 00:37:01.160
You may be familiar
with recent stories

00:37:01.160 --> 00:37:05.090
coming out of the most
elite prep schools

00:37:05.090 --> 00:37:08.120
in the country, the Dalton
School or the United Nations

00:37:08.120 --> 00:37:11.330
International school in New York
City or the Brentwood School

00:37:11.330 --> 00:37:15.080
in Los Angeles each of which,
again, are the most elite prep

00:37:15.080 --> 00:37:19.190
schools in America with
the price tags to prove it

00:37:19.190 --> 00:37:22.160
and each has been
completely overtaken

00:37:22.160 --> 00:37:25.490
by a particularly aggressive
and destructive form

00:37:25.490 --> 00:37:28.264
of social justice.

00:37:28.264 --> 00:37:30.420
I'm sorry to say that
in my view there's

00:37:30.420 --> 00:37:33.150
certainly very little
use in crying out

00:37:33.150 --> 00:37:35.640
for liberty in response.

00:37:35.640 --> 00:37:39.270
With American liberty,
there's always been

00:37:39.270 --> 00:37:41.310
and indeed there
must be definitions

00:37:41.310 --> 00:37:46.290
which prescribes certain
behaviors and proscribe others.

00:37:46.290 --> 00:37:49.750
For example, Christian elites
in their day banned obscenity

00:37:49.750 --> 00:37:51.720
and for a brief period,
right, even banned

00:37:51.720 --> 00:37:53.760
the consumption of alcohol.

00:37:53.760 --> 00:37:56.940
The gentlemen of their day
put Eugene Debs in prison

00:37:56.940 --> 00:38:01.140
and defined homosexuality
as a mental illness.

00:38:01.140 --> 00:38:05.550
The meritocratz, they
enforced standardized testing

00:38:05.550 --> 00:38:08.700
and imposed globalization
and deindustrialization

00:38:08.700 --> 00:38:10.470
on the American economy.

00:38:10.470 --> 00:38:12.480
Thus when social
justice elites today

00:38:12.480 --> 00:38:17.190
prescribe hate speech, when
they redefine the word sex, when

00:38:17.190 --> 00:38:22.268
they set out to racialize all of
American society, in one sense,

00:38:22.268 --> 00:38:24.060
they're doing what
elites have always done.

00:38:24.060 --> 00:38:27.120
They're promulgating
their own class culture

00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:31.840
and defining it as normative
for the rest of society.

00:38:31.840 --> 00:38:34.670
So therefore, my
contention here tonight

00:38:34.670 --> 00:38:38.450
is that we shouldn't lament
the existence of an elite

00:38:38.450 --> 00:38:40.070
or the power of
that elite to define

00:38:40.070 --> 00:38:42.650
social norms through the
force of custom and law.

00:38:42.650 --> 00:38:46.970
This is ubiquitous
to human society.

00:38:46.970 --> 00:38:48.740
What we should do
instead is eliminate

00:38:48.740 --> 00:38:52.280
the existence of this
elite, this culture,

00:38:52.280 --> 00:38:56.120
this exercise of power.

00:38:56.120 --> 00:39:01.010
Recall Jefferson's view of
the natural aristocracy.

00:39:01.010 --> 00:39:05.030
In his words, this was,
quote, "the most precious gift

00:39:05.030 --> 00:39:10.160
of nature for the instruction,
the trusts, and government

00:39:10.160 --> 00:39:12.660
of society," unquote.

00:39:12.660 --> 00:39:14.720
For even in a
democratic republic,

00:39:14.720 --> 00:39:17.780
the question of rulership
is not obviated.

00:39:17.780 --> 00:39:21.860
The need for coordination to
accomplish some good combined

00:39:21.860 --> 00:39:25.670
with the frailty of
mankind indicates as much.

00:39:25.670 --> 00:39:30.890
Thus a true aristocracy should
be both desired and cultivated

00:39:30.890 --> 00:39:35.000
both in its talents
and in its virtue

00:39:35.000 --> 00:39:39.210
precisely so that the
whole may benefit.

00:39:39.210 --> 00:39:40.911
How to do this?

00:39:40.911 --> 00:39:47.550
This is our great task not
simply for today but always.

00:39:47.550 --> 00:39:51.060
Shall we work through
national party politics?

00:39:51.060 --> 00:39:54.480
Shall we pursue an invigorated
form of federalism?

00:39:54.480 --> 00:39:57.870
Shall we seek to separate
the country into pillars

00:39:57.870 --> 00:40:00.150
as was done after World
War II and countries

00:40:00.150 --> 00:40:03.750
like the Netherlands and
Belgium where each community had

00:40:03.750 --> 00:40:07.740
its own institutions of media
and sports clubs and schools

00:40:07.740 --> 00:40:12.060
and universities and hospitals
and political parties?

00:40:12.060 --> 00:40:14.220
I take these to be
practical questions

00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:18.090
that people of goodwill can
certainly debate and differ on.

00:40:18.090 --> 00:40:20.860
What we should not
differ on however,

00:40:20.860 --> 00:40:25.290
is the cultivation of a counter
elite that knows and pursues

00:40:25.290 --> 00:40:30.990
the transcendentals of
truth, beauty, and goodness.

00:40:30.990 --> 00:40:33.530
Being spiritual,
these transcendentals

00:40:33.530 --> 00:40:37.380
are none the diminished for
being shared and, in fact,

00:40:37.380 --> 00:40:40.200
only enhanced in their sharing.

00:40:40.200 --> 00:40:44.520
Thus these are truly
common to society,

00:40:44.520 --> 00:40:47.550
the dignity of
every human being,

00:40:47.550 --> 00:40:50.970
the dissemination of
knowledge, of the reality that

00:40:50.970 --> 00:40:55.530
exist beyond us, the cultivation
of reason, the propagation

00:40:55.530 --> 00:40:58.320
and diffusion of beauty.

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:01.560
The successes of the
social justice faith,

00:41:01.560 --> 00:41:03.870
such as they are,
point up the fact

00:41:03.870 --> 00:41:07.440
that mankind yearns for
a collective project

00:41:07.440 --> 00:41:11.010
through which he can pass beyond
the baseness of this world

00:41:11.010 --> 00:41:12.910
and into another.

00:41:12.910 --> 00:41:17.460
The meritocrats seemingly
have satisfied none, not even

00:41:17.460 --> 00:41:19.080
themselves.

00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:22.040
Success for its own
sake, accomplishment

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:24.390
turned toward
individual gain, virtue

00:41:24.390 --> 00:41:27.930
as a series of leadership
opportunities completed

00:41:27.930 --> 00:41:30.000
have not satisfied.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:32.040
Social justice
adherence know as well

00:41:32.040 --> 00:41:35.130
as we do that mankind
is made for something

00:41:35.130 --> 00:41:37.470
greater than what we see.

00:41:37.470 --> 00:41:39.210
Satisfying such a
yearning with something

00:41:39.210 --> 00:41:41.820
that brings true
happiness to mankind

00:41:41.820 --> 00:41:44.440
is the only thing
that can answer it.

00:41:44.440 --> 00:41:45.187
Thank you.

00:41:45.187 --> 00:41:47.672
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:54.992 --> 00:41:56.700
SPEAKER 1: We now have
time for some Q&amp;A.

00:41:56.700 --> 00:41:58.320
If you have a question,
please make your way

00:41:58.320 --> 00:41:59.403
to one of the microphones.

00:42:11.255 --> 00:42:12.630
DAREL E. PAUL: If
you don't talk,

00:42:12.630 --> 00:42:14.580
you're going to force me to
talk for another 20 minutes,

00:42:14.580 --> 00:42:15.580
and you don't want that.

00:42:30.210 --> 00:42:31.710
AUDIENCE: Thank
you for speaking.

00:42:31.710 --> 00:42:34.110
You mentioned that we--

00:42:34.110 --> 00:42:36.240
you suggested this or
put forward the idea

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:40.170
that we need to
raise up a new elite.

00:42:40.170 --> 00:42:44.130
What do you think of
the idea of the grifters

00:42:44.130 --> 00:42:51.120
or like a deceptive elite or a
tool used by the current elite

00:42:51.120 --> 00:42:56.740
to misdirect or misguide
a potentially new rising

00:42:56.740 --> 00:43:00.180
elite that could counter
them but misleading them

00:43:00.180 --> 00:43:01.320
by deception?

00:43:01.320 --> 00:43:06.180
You see a lot of this in
potentially like Koch brothers

00:43:06.180 --> 00:43:08.730
founded organizations
and things of that nature

00:43:08.730 --> 00:43:17.250
where people are led into rather
than using the institutions

00:43:17.250 --> 00:43:19.860
as promoting virtue
but as promoting

00:43:19.860 --> 00:43:23.400
a kind of liberty that
is more laissez faire

00:43:23.400 --> 00:43:27.810
or promoting like
freedom to do non--

00:43:27.810 --> 00:43:30.220
promote non-virtue and
things of that nature.

00:43:30.220 --> 00:43:31.962
What do you think of that?

00:43:31.962 --> 00:43:33.420
DAREL E. PAUL: I
mean, I think this

00:43:33.420 --> 00:43:36.240
is one of the great
questions for what

00:43:36.240 --> 00:43:38.160
we might call it the
right, conservatives,

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:39.060
whatever it might be.

00:43:41.850 --> 00:43:45.390
What I think characterizes
the American elite today is

00:43:45.390 --> 00:43:48.690
certainly a very left-wing
attitude shall we say

00:43:48.690 --> 00:43:52.470
on cultural issues, but actually
quite moderate to maybe from,

00:43:52.470 --> 00:43:57.120
perhaps, so-called conservative
attitude towards economics--

00:43:57.120 --> 00:44:02.350
free trade, globalization, open
borders, these sorts of things.

00:44:02.350 --> 00:44:08.400
So I think that the older system
in which conservatives argue

00:44:08.400 --> 00:44:13.260
for the free market is not
particularly threatening

00:44:13.260 --> 00:44:15.150
and in fact can be
incorporated in many ways

00:44:15.150 --> 00:44:20.130
into the kinds of elite
thought that we have today.

00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:21.370
There are some differences.

00:44:21.370 --> 00:44:23.730
I don't want to downplay
them completely,

00:44:23.730 --> 00:44:27.030
but I don't think
it would constitute

00:44:27.030 --> 00:44:29.100
any kind of counter elite.

00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:32.970
And so certainly the aspects
of globalization that we see

00:44:32.970 --> 00:44:35.400
is something that's
quite amenable to what

00:44:35.400 --> 00:44:38.010
we take to the left as well
as to much of the right today.

00:44:38.010 --> 00:44:40.500
And this is where I think
the Trump presidency was

00:44:40.500 --> 00:44:43.830
most distinct in terms
of its foreign policy

00:44:43.830 --> 00:44:47.460
on trade, on immigration,
on war, which

00:44:47.460 --> 00:44:50.030
I fear we're going to see quite
a reversal under the Biden

00:44:50.030 --> 00:44:50.655
administration.

00:44:54.050 --> 00:44:56.960
AUDIENCE: Thank you for coming
and speaking today, Dr. Paul.

00:44:56.960 --> 00:45:00.900
My question is you say
that we need this counter

00:45:00.900 --> 00:45:04.100
elite to pursue the true
and the good and knowledge

00:45:04.100 --> 00:45:10.810
and things like that, but my
question more is do we even

00:45:10.810 --> 00:45:15.040
think that whether you raise up
that good or not it still has--

00:45:15.040 --> 00:45:17.290
that elite it still
has to be somewhat

00:45:17.290 --> 00:45:20.320
popular to get in power
and places of power,

00:45:20.320 --> 00:45:24.650
and do you think that it even
can be popular even more?

00:45:24.650 --> 00:45:25.780
And if so how?

00:45:28.690 --> 00:45:32.560
DAREL E. PAUL: I think
it's a good question,

00:45:32.560 --> 00:45:36.290
and I think there are a couple
of different ways to answer it.

00:45:36.290 --> 00:45:39.550
One is to obviously
focus on institutions

00:45:39.550 --> 00:45:42.220
and to suggest that
perhaps it is not

00:45:42.220 --> 00:45:44.440
popular at a national
level, but it

00:45:44.440 --> 00:45:45.950
may be popular at local levels.

00:45:45.950 --> 00:45:49.900
And so there may be institutions
at levels of state government

00:45:49.900 --> 00:45:51.530
or there may be
institutions in terms

00:45:51.530 --> 00:45:53.530
of particular kinds of
professional institutions

00:45:53.530 --> 00:45:56.860
that might be able
to be locations

00:45:56.860 --> 00:45:59.680
in which this new
elite can form itself.

00:45:59.680 --> 00:46:01.630
And certainly when
we see the experience

00:46:01.630 --> 00:46:05.470
of the social justice
elite that we have now,

00:46:05.470 --> 00:46:07.900
that was not particularly
popular at its time

00:46:07.900 --> 00:46:10.090
and may not even be
that popular today.

00:46:10.090 --> 00:46:12.160
It's difficult to tell.

00:46:12.160 --> 00:46:16.030
But there is the
occupation of positions

00:46:16.030 --> 00:46:18.550
of power and authority
and institutions

00:46:18.550 --> 00:46:21.660
and then the use of those
positions of institutions

00:46:21.660 --> 00:46:27.730
to propagate views, and
so I guess much of it

00:46:27.730 --> 00:46:32.640
comes down to the boundaries,
if you will, of democracy.

00:46:32.640 --> 00:46:34.390
And if one takes the
United States to have

00:46:34.390 --> 00:46:36.550
been a democratic republic
for its entire history,

00:46:36.550 --> 00:46:39.460
I think we still have seen
many kinds of elite projects

00:46:39.460 --> 00:46:41.470
that have not been
particularly popular

00:46:41.470 --> 00:46:45.370
but still have been instantiated
because of elite power,

00:46:45.370 --> 00:46:49.240
and so therefore I think
popularity in and of itself

00:46:49.240 --> 00:46:52.866
is not necessarily an
impediment to the cultivation

00:46:52.866 --> 00:46:54.283
of accountability
a counter elite.

00:46:59.220 --> 00:47:01.500
AUDIENCE: Thank you
kindly for your time.

00:47:01.500 --> 00:47:04.560
As you yourself identified
in your definition of terms,

00:47:04.560 --> 00:47:06.910
the precision of language
is incredibly important,

00:47:06.910 --> 00:47:10.350
especially in this discussion
within the public square.

00:47:10.350 --> 00:47:13.980
So my question is, in this
rising of the new elite, how

00:47:13.980 --> 00:47:17.400
do you recommend that once the
new elite themselves identifies

00:47:17.400 --> 00:47:20.670
and understands what certain
terms mean, specifically terms

00:47:20.670 --> 00:47:24.450
like social justice and
truth, as they're misused

00:47:24.450 --> 00:47:28.050
and misdefined on
the alternate side

00:47:28.050 --> 00:47:31.380
how should the new
elite seek to permeate

00:47:31.380 --> 00:47:37.080
the culture with the correct
definitions, if you will.

00:47:37.080 --> 00:47:40.270
DAREL E. PAUL: I mean, this
is quite a difficult task,

00:47:40.270 --> 00:47:43.710
because if one is trying
to counter definitions

00:47:43.710 --> 00:47:46.320
with alternative ones.

00:47:46.320 --> 00:47:48.660
The possibility, of
course, one begins

00:47:48.660 --> 00:47:52.270
speaking a different
language in essence.

00:47:52.270 --> 00:47:55.990
And so I saw something similar.

00:47:55.990 --> 00:48:00.370
So two years ago, a
little over two years ago,

00:48:00.370 --> 00:48:03.810
there was a social
justice flare up,

00:48:03.810 --> 00:48:05.940
shall we say, at my institution.

00:48:05.940 --> 00:48:09.120
And much of the dispute
was around the definition

00:48:09.120 --> 00:48:12.510
of words like
violence, and I found

00:48:12.510 --> 00:48:15.690
that it was extremely
difficult to talk to people

00:48:15.690 --> 00:48:17.877
about what violence is.

00:48:17.877 --> 00:48:19.710
But I think at the same
time it's essential,

00:48:19.710 --> 00:48:24.900
because much of, I think,
social justice ideology

00:48:24.900 --> 00:48:29.640
uses terms that have
particular kinds of meanings,

00:48:29.640 --> 00:48:31.300
and people don't
question those meaning

00:48:31.300 --> 00:48:34.380
and so, therefore, are
willing to, shall we say,

00:48:34.380 --> 00:48:38.100
give the benefit of the doubt to
people whose usage of language

00:48:38.100 --> 00:48:41.010
should not have the
benefit of the doubt.

00:48:41.010 --> 00:48:44.670
Some of the most hardcore are
not going to be persuaded.

00:48:44.670 --> 00:48:46.350
They are not persuadable.

00:48:46.350 --> 00:48:48.000
But I think there is--

00:48:48.000 --> 00:48:50.010
at least my experience
certainly suggests--

00:48:50.010 --> 00:48:55.200
a much larger middle group
that is either persuadable

00:48:55.200 --> 00:48:59.610
or certainly
reachable and can be

00:48:59.610 --> 00:49:04.980
talked to and with on these
topics, because I think they

00:49:04.980 --> 00:49:05.580
are worried.

00:49:05.580 --> 00:49:11.070
They are concerned by the power
of the social justice ideology,

00:49:11.070 --> 00:49:14.370
but there are such pressures
upon people to conform.

00:49:14.370 --> 00:49:18.240
And I'm not sure how it is
here at Hillsdale College

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:21.060
but certainly at Williams
College-- it's a similar place,

00:49:21.060 --> 00:49:24.060
a rather small liberal
arts college, very remote,

00:49:24.060 --> 00:49:25.590
kind of in the
middle of nowhere--

00:49:25.590 --> 00:49:28.920
there's a tremendous amount
of social pressure to conform,

00:49:28.920 --> 00:49:30.960
because you want to
have friends and you

00:49:30.960 --> 00:49:32.550
want to be well thought of.

00:49:32.550 --> 00:49:35.830
And so it takes some
degree of courage, I think.

00:49:35.830 --> 00:49:38.760
And so that would
be a piece of advice

00:49:38.760 --> 00:49:40.815
that I would give as
well to have courage.

00:49:43.333 --> 00:49:44.750
AUDIENCE: Thank
you for your talk.

00:49:44.750 --> 00:49:46.720
This is sort of similar
to that question

00:49:46.720 --> 00:49:48.250
but on a slightly
different note.

00:49:48.250 --> 00:49:51.460
How do you think that the
increased use of social media

00:49:51.460 --> 00:49:54.880
to propagate some of
these ideas has impacted

00:49:54.880 --> 00:49:57.640
the rise of the newly,
and is it worth it at all

00:49:57.640 --> 00:50:01.480
to engage on that platform
to help with bringing people

00:50:01.480 --> 00:50:04.960
to understanding more correct
definitions about things

00:50:04.960 --> 00:50:07.330
like social justice?

00:50:07.330 --> 00:50:09.190
DAREL E. PAUL: So
I've seen data that

00:50:09.190 --> 00:50:12.790
suggests that when it comes
at least to political topics,

00:50:12.790 --> 00:50:17.590
social media is pretty
heavily weighted on the left.

00:50:17.590 --> 00:50:20.830
For whatever reasons
we could speculate on--

00:50:20.830 --> 00:50:24.430
I'm sure there's probably
research on this as well--

00:50:24.430 --> 00:50:27.010
there tends to be a more
kind of left orientation

00:50:27.010 --> 00:50:29.920
on social media.

00:50:29.920 --> 00:50:32.950
My sense is a social
media is a platform built

00:50:32.950 --> 00:50:38.987
for emotional outbursts
and diatribes,

00:50:38.987 --> 00:50:41.320
and so therefore it doesn't
lend particularly well to we

00:50:41.320 --> 00:50:42.487
might say reasoned argument.

00:50:42.487 --> 00:50:47.140
But what it can do
also is be a medium

00:50:47.140 --> 00:50:50.470
of symbols and symbolism.

00:50:50.470 --> 00:50:55.450
So rather than engaging
people in argument,

00:50:55.450 --> 00:50:57.460
I think what might
be better is to--

00:50:57.460 --> 00:50:59.800
well, you guys are far
better at this than I--

00:50:59.800 --> 00:51:01.150
share memes.

00:51:01.150 --> 00:51:03.790
Use humor, mock.

00:51:03.790 --> 00:51:06.600
One thing that people who
are deadly, deadly serious,

00:51:06.600 --> 00:51:08.290
as most social
justice people are--

00:51:08.290 --> 00:51:11.560
they tend not to have a very
well-developed sense of humor--

00:51:11.560 --> 00:51:15.880
is to make fun of them because
it is something they absolutely

00:51:15.880 --> 00:51:17.560
cannot bear.

00:51:17.560 --> 00:51:20.570
And so I'm guessing
you know of places,

00:51:20.570 --> 00:51:23.050
websites like the Babylon
Bee, which is very good.

00:51:23.050 --> 00:51:25.480
Sometimes their articles don't
live up to the headlines,

00:51:25.480 --> 00:51:28.780
but by God those
headlines are 90% the time

00:51:28.780 --> 00:51:31.957
absolutely hilarious.

00:51:31.957 --> 00:51:33.790
Those kinds of things,
I think, can actually

00:51:33.790 --> 00:51:36.550
be probably more
productive than trying

00:51:36.550 --> 00:51:42.830
to take the high ground
engage in reasoned debate.

00:51:42.830 --> 00:51:45.620
AUDIENCE: Hi, thank you
so much for your time.

00:51:45.620 --> 00:51:49.870
I was just wondering
since social justice says

00:51:49.870 --> 00:51:56.050
that the unoppressed can
never fully learn or know

00:51:56.050 --> 00:51:58.420
what it means to
be oppressed, do

00:51:58.420 --> 00:52:03.460
you think that inserting social
justice into the education

00:52:03.460 --> 00:52:10.390
system kind of promotes an
attitude of deserved ignorance

00:52:10.390 --> 00:52:13.600
in a way, and it
actually stunts people

00:52:13.600 --> 00:52:18.550
from reaching across the aisle
and learning about cultures

00:52:18.550 --> 00:52:22.810
and experiences and basically
that it's a social justice--

00:52:22.810 --> 00:52:26.170
Do you think it's a
self-defeating concept?

00:52:26.170 --> 00:52:27.910
DAREL E. PAUL: I
guess in part it

00:52:27.910 --> 00:52:31.300
depends on what we think the
goal of social justice is.

00:52:31.300 --> 00:52:36.970
If we think it is a more
integrated society, one that

00:52:36.970 --> 00:52:41.890
is characterized by kind of a
flourishing of understanding

00:52:41.890 --> 00:52:44.170
and sharing across
groups, then I

00:52:44.170 --> 00:52:46.300
think that's very much not
what it's accomplishing.

00:52:46.300 --> 00:52:49.630
Certainly, it seems to be
doing exactly the opposite.

00:52:49.630 --> 00:52:51.730
That being said,
I'm personally not

00:52:51.730 --> 00:52:53.320
convinced that that's its goal.

00:52:53.320 --> 00:52:57.490
I think its goal is much
more the exercise of power

00:52:57.490 --> 00:53:01.300
and to transform
the current elite

00:53:01.300 --> 00:53:05.710
and to provide places for
they themselves to occupy.

00:53:05.710 --> 00:53:09.520
When we look at the incident
in The New York Times,

00:53:09.520 --> 00:53:12.220
for example, in the spring
around the whole Tom Cotton

00:53:12.220 --> 00:53:16.450
editorial where the name escapes
me but the editor of the Times

00:53:16.450 --> 00:53:20.110
was essentially
fired over that, it

00:53:20.110 --> 00:53:21.610
was a way to open
up space and allow

00:53:21.610 --> 00:53:23.290
new people to come
in and take positions

00:53:23.290 --> 00:53:24.610
of power and authority.

00:53:24.610 --> 00:53:26.977
If we think that that's
the fundamental goal,

00:53:26.977 --> 00:53:28.810
then we shouldn't be
surprised that it's not

00:53:28.810 --> 00:53:31.780
lending to the kinds of
conversations, if you will,

00:53:31.780 --> 00:53:36.520
that it claims to want to have.

00:53:36.520 --> 00:53:40.210
One of the things that I
think is the most certainly

00:53:40.210 --> 00:53:43.690
depressing and maybe if I was in
a particularly pessimistic mood

00:53:43.690 --> 00:53:48.646
dangerous aspects of social
justice is it's racialism.

00:53:48.646 --> 00:53:52.360
That is that there is
really no human experience,

00:53:52.360 --> 00:53:54.220
and I think the speaker
at 4 o'clock today

00:53:54.220 --> 00:53:57.280
spoke very eloquently on
so many of these aspects

00:53:57.280 --> 00:53:59.090
of the human experience.

00:53:59.090 --> 00:54:01.600
And social justice
tends to eliminate

00:54:01.600 --> 00:54:03.580
those kinds of conversations.

00:54:03.580 --> 00:54:07.720
My hope certainly
is that by engaging

00:54:07.720 --> 00:54:13.210
in those conversations, one can
find probably not very hard,

00:54:13.210 --> 00:54:17.265
in not a very difficult way
that kind of common humanity,

00:54:17.265 --> 00:54:19.390
because that is what I
would hope that we would all

00:54:19.390 --> 00:54:22.810
want to gather around,
certainly some kind

00:54:22.810 --> 00:54:25.420
of intercultural sharing
and understanding

00:54:25.420 --> 00:54:31.255
but underneath that
common human experience.

00:54:31.255 --> 00:54:34.030
SPEAKER 2: We have time
for one more question.

00:54:34.030 --> 00:54:36.200
AUDIENCE: Hi, thank
you for speaking today.

00:54:36.200 --> 00:54:36.700
I'm curious.

00:54:36.700 --> 00:54:39.910
We've been talking to both about
public opinion and then also

00:54:39.910 --> 00:54:43.330
about the, kind of, character
of the elite classes,

00:54:43.330 --> 00:54:45.727
and I'm kind of
curious about which one

00:54:45.727 --> 00:54:47.560
do you think is more
important or like which

00:54:47.560 --> 00:54:48.630
follows from the other.

00:54:48.630 --> 00:54:51.640
Like, does the character of the
elite affect public opinion,

00:54:51.640 --> 00:54:54.580
or does like public opinion
and common perceptions

00:54:54.580 --> 00:54:58.120
affect the character
of the elite?

00:54:58.120 --> 00:55:00.460
DAREL E. PAUL: I'm sad to
say that the social science

00:55:00.460 --> 00:55:03.640
research seems to suggest that
it's elite opinion creates

00:55:03.640 --> 00:55:05.200
public opinion.

00:55:05.200 --> 00:55:08.800
In fact, a friend of
mine in my department

00:55:08.800 --> 00:55:12.070
showed me some research
within the last five years

00:55:12.070 --> 00:55:15.130
or so that seems to have
pretty good empirical support

00:55:15.130 --> 00:55:20.408
that people look to those who
they take to be their leaders.

00:55:20.408 --> 00:55:21.950
They don't have
opinions on subjects,

00:55:21.950 --> 00:55:24.970
but they look to those leaders
to have those opinions.

00:55:24.970 --> 00:55:27.760
And so it is for the
most part the elites that

00:55:27.760 --> 00:55:31.360
craft the opinions
that the public has,

00:55:31.360 --> 00:55:33.220
not fully, not completely.

00:55:33.220 --> 00:55:36.640
I don't want to go down too much
of a kind of a Thorstein Veblen

00:55:36.640 --> 00:55:39.243
sociology in which
the cultural values

00:55:39.243 --> 00:55:40.660
the top are just
kind of flow down

00:55:40.660 --> 00:55:42.610
or are forced down
upon the lowers

00:55:42.610 --> 00:55:45.250
or of Pierre Bourdieu
or someone like that.

00:55:45.250 --> 00:55:48.730
I think there is some
dimension of autonomy

00:55:48.730 --> 00:55:51.820
among middle classes
and working classes,

00:55:51.820 --> 00:55:55.220
but those autonomous
views, opinions,

00:55:55.220 --> 00:55:59.290
values are very difficult
to express politically.

00:55:59.290 --> 00:56:03.790
And so this is why I think the
project of elites is one that

00:56:03.790 --> 00:56:08.350
we cannot avoid and one that's
desperately important to engage

00:56:08.350 --> 00:56:10.400
in and not to simply
give up and say, well,

00:56:10.400 --> 00:56:12.580
those are the people that
run the American Medical

00:56:12.580 --> 00:56:15.370
Association and the American
Legal Association and much

00:56:15.370 --> 00:56:16.390
of higher education.

00:56:16.390 --> 00:56:17.900
And nothing we can do about it.

00:56:17.900 --> 00:56:20.422
But at least here
we can have whatever

00:56:20.422 --> 00:56:22.630
we want to believe in my
church or in my neighborhood

00:56:22.630 --> 00:56:23.830
or whatever it is.

00:56:23.830 --> 00:56:28.098
Because those smaller,
more local kinds of spaces

00:56:28.098 --> 00:56:29.390
are not going to be left alone.

00:56:29.390 --> 00:56:31.840
I think we can see that already
in just the first couple

00:56:31.840 --> 00:56:33.820
of weeks of the
Biden administration.

00:56:33.820 --> 00:56:36.940
And so the project
of forming an elite

00:56:36.940 --> 00:56:39.580
is an unavoidable one
and one that a place

00:56:39.580 --> 00:56:43.492
like Hillsdale I take embraces.

00:56:43.492 --> 00:56:45.450
SPEAKER 1: Please join
me in thanking Dr. Paul.

00:56:45.450 --> 00:56:48.500
[APPLAUSE]

